<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelusium</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell el-Farama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelusium was a city at the easternmost mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, near modern Port Said, guarding the route into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Invading armies often fought there: the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> nearby in 525 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a> in 343 BCE. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> was murdered on the shore at Pelusium in 48 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelusium.jpg" alt="Pelusium" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg: Jeff Dahl derivative work: MinisterForBadTimes (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Egypt-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
